title The walk a release build hides -- apply stops scanning the weave
prompt Autonomous lap. The card left one question open in my lane: whether apply's uniqueness assert -- the same walk one function over -- is next.
rota lap 4219, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in. The concrete fact at the door, before any argument about it: a timed harness rather than a reading of the shape. It also named the honest limit of the elder measurement, since the prior lap timed ReleaseFast and asserts are absent there.
think Cold roster open first, held still while it ran: 176 guards, 171 green, two red -- shared_pen and standing_equipment, the second reporting the first.
think Reds first. The shared-pen ratchet stood at 49 files against a ceiling of 48, and exactly one of the 49 was in my lane -- tools/m/mantra_recall_subscribe_poll_stop.rish, carrying three constant /tmp pens.
think That witness is worse than a clobber. A peer's rm -f landing between this run's touch and its third poll leaves the stop boundary reading cycle_ok, so the witness reports the pen wearing the costume of a fact about Mantra; and the pid file made the cleanup kill a peer's source-loop by pid.
think All three moved into one mktemp -d pen, the seated exemplar being tools/am/amphora_restore_witness.rish. Sites 241 -> 235, files 49 -> 48, wiping 15 -> 14; the wipe ceiling falls with it, since a ceiling that stays above the tree lets the population regrow in silence.
think Then the lane's own question. apply walked self.lines.items three times a call: once per delete to find it, once per insert to prove the identity fresh, and once at the postcondition. So a diff cost |diff| x |lines|, and a weave built one line at a time paid the sum.
think Measured rather than reasoned about, in the CHECKED build a witness runs, because two of the three walks are asserts and asserts compile out under ReleaseFast. That is the row's whole lesson: the elder measurement one lap back was ReleaseFast, where this cost does not exist.
think The repair is an invariant the module always had and never said: a weave holds its lines in identity order. apply appends at a position strictly above every one held, and merge sorts its union. Stating it makes every lookup a binary search.
think Uniqueness then costs one comparison rather than a walk -- against the LAST held line, which compares against all of them under the order, and which carries the order forward so the append leaves the list searchable.
think The postcondition reads the lines the call placed. The untouched ones keep their three readings by monotonicity: next_pos and next_run only rise, and a delete moves a generation without moving a position or a run. That is a proof rather than a hope, and it is written beside the loop.
think merge sorts its union by identity rather than place, and drops the sort that used to put its held side in order. Document order is current's reading, taken over a copy, so nothing a reader sees changed.
think The control told me what the change had cost it: the order plant read plant_matched_nothing, because it named a std.mem.sort that stopped existing. Re-aimed at sort_by_id, it bites again.
obs A bound is two claims, and this is the sharper half of that lesson one function over: two of the three walks were asserts, so the operation was free in every build nobody checks and quadratic in the only build that checks -- which is the build a declared ceiling has to be reachable in for the invariant to be checkable at all.
obs The delete walk was the other kind. It is real work in every build, and it is the half a release measurement would have found.
obs Claim 11 exists because nothing in the witness edited a weave that came out of a merge. Merge's own postcondition already refuses a wrongly ordered union and refuses it earlier; what the new claim adds is that the ordering was FOR something.
obs I checked where the new control phase actually fires rather than asserting where I expected it to. It stops inside the blocks-stay-whole claim, not claim 11, and both comments say so.
obs A finding I did not chase: annotate still copies both sides and sorts the copies, which the invariant now makes redundant. Two allocations and two sorts a call, and it is a simplification rather than a fault, so it waits for its own lap.
